window.docContents[46].resolve({"/Tier-3___-trusted___-not-read/AArch64-semantics/Arm64.Trusted.Syscalls.Mm.Madvise/#file-Arm64___Trusted___Syscalls___Mm___Madvise":{"contents":"Paper › arm64-semantics/Arm64/Trusted/Syscalls/Mm › Madvise.leanTier 3 · arm64-semantics\n\nTier 3 · arm64-semantics used by the symbolic proof 2 headline theorems depend on it 88 of 126 lines trusted source ↗Trusted, reviewed with the engine. The AArch64 model in arm64-semantics is Tier 3: it is reviewed in its own repository (check-trust-guards). The declarations that the headline statements use are shown for completeness.\n\nDeclarations on this page (14)def zeroNonFrozendef madvFreeNonFrozendef MADV_NORMALdef MADV_RANDOMdef MADV_SEQUENTIALdef MADV_WILLNEEDdef MADV_DONTNEEDdef MADV_FREEdef MADV_HUGEPAGEdef MADV_NOHUGEPAGEdef MADV_DONTDUMPdef MADV_DODUMPdef SYS_MADVISEdef execMadvise\n\nLinux Virtual Memory madvise SyscallModels the Linux 7.3 madvise (__NR_madvise = 233) syscall (mm/madvise.c:2003 do_madvise), including synchronous MADV_DONTNEED zero-fill (Memory.zeroNonFrozen), asynchronous MADV_FREE lazy-reclaim marking (Memory.madvFreeNonFrozen, mm/madvise.c:808 and mm/rmap.c:2078), and no-op behavioral, transparent-hugepage, and coredump advice constants (mm/madvise.c:1425, include/uapi/asm-generic/mman-common.h:42).\n\n\n\ndef zeroNonFrozenL31–41 ↗#Zero-fill all non-frozen mapped bytes in [base, base + len), clearing lazyFree := false and preserving their protection bits (m.perm) and anon state; frozen and unmapped bytes (and bytes outside the range) are unchanged. Used for madvise(..., MADV_DONTNEED) on private anonymous memory (mm/madvise.c:936 madvise_dontneed_free, which synchronously zaps PTEs so subsequent faults allocate fresh zero pages). Never touches frozen bytes.\n\ndef madvFreeNonFrozenL43–54 ↗#Mark all non-frozen mapped bytes in [base, base + len) with lazyFree := true, preserving their protection bits (m.perm), anon state, and byte values; frozen and unmapped bytes (and bytes outside the range) are unchanged. Used for madvise(..., MADV_FREE) on private anonymous memory (mm/madvise.c:808 madvise_free_single_vma, where Linux 7.3 marks folios lazyfree so ttu_anon_lazyfree_folio may asynchronously reclaim unwritten pages to zero at any later point until dirtied by a store). Never touches frozen bytes.\n\n\n\ndef MADV_NORMALL58–59 ↗#Linux madvise advice constants (include/uapi/asm-generic/mman-common.h:42-70).\n\ndef MADV_RANDOML60 ↗#\n\ndef MADV_SEQUENTIALL61 ↗#\n\ndef MADV_WILLNEEDL62 ↗#\n\ndef MADV_DONTNEEDL63 ↗#\n\ndef MADV_FREEL64 ↗#\n\ndef MADV_HUGEPAGEL65 ↗#\n\ndef MADV_NOHUGEPAGEL66 ↗#\n\ndef MADV_DONTDUMPL67 ↗#\n\ndef MADV_DODUMPL68 ↗#\n\ndef SYS_MADVISEL70–71 ↗#Linux AArch64 madvise syscall number (__NR_madvise = 233, include/uapi/asm-generic/unistd.h:642, mm/madvise.c:2003).\n\ndef execMadviseL73–127 ↗#madvise(addr, len, advice) (__NR_madvise = 233, mm/madvise.c:2003 do_madvise) on non-frozen mapped memory. Models:* MADV_DONTNEED (4): synchronously zero-fills private anonymous memory and clears lazyFree;* MADV_FREE (8): marks private anonymous memory lazyFree := true so reading before an intervening store fails closed (.Exception, preventing proofs from assuming load1 == load2 across asynchronous ttu_anon_lazyfree_folio reclaim on Linux 7.3);* pure behavioral, transparent-hugepage (VM_HUGEPAGE / VM_NOHUGEPAGE), and coredump hints (MADV_NORMAL = 0, MADV_RANDOM = 1, MADV_SEQUENTIAL = 2, MADV_WILLNEED = 3, MADV_HUGEPAGE = 14, MADV_NOHUGEPAGE = 15, MADV_DONTDUMP = 16, MADV_DODUMP = 17);* [INTENTIONAL_SCOPE]: MADV_HUGEPAGE = 14 and MADV_NOHUGEPAGE = 15 (mm/huge_memory.c / mm/khugepaged.c hugepage_madvise) assume a CONFIG_TRANSPARENT_HUGEPAGE=y Linux 7.3 kernel profile on normal user memory (!VM_IO && !VM_PFNMAP && !VM_DAX), where setting or clearing VM_HUGEPAGE / VM_NOHUGEPAGE always succeeds with 0 (whereas a kernel built with CONFIG_TRANSPARENT_HUGEPAGE=n or special device VMAs would return -EINVAL);* [INTENTIONAL_SCOPE]: MADV_DONTNEED and MADV_FREE on non-anonymous (!isAnon, e.g. file-backed ELF .data) memory fail closed with .Exception \"madvise on non-anonymous memory\" (on Linux 7.3, MADV_FREE returns -EINVAL when !vma_is_anonymous in mm/madvise.c:821, and MADV_DONTNEED re-faults underlying file bytes instead of zeroing); all other advice values fail closed with .Exception \"unmodeled madvise advice\".\n\n\n\n","context":"AlphaProof Code: Verifying Signal’s Security at the Machine-Code Level","header":"arm64-semantics/Arm64/Trusted/Syscalls/Mm/Madvise.lean","id":"/Tier-3___-trusted___-not-read/AArch64-semantics/Arm64.Trusted.Syscalls.Mm.Madvise/#file-Arm64___Trusted___Syscalls___Mm___Madvise"}});